WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.181
A more common means of high-speed Internet connection for both residential and business customers is Digital Subscriber Link, or DSL.

00:08.181 --> 00:17.174
With DSL, a customer has a dedicated phone line from the origin to a telco office. The closer a customer is to the office, the faster the connection.

00:17.174 --> 00:26.332
For this reason, many people cannot get DSL because they are not close enough to a telco office. There are two types of DSL connections you need to know about for the exam.

00:26.332 --> 00:36.781
SDSL, or Synchronous DSL, in this form of DSL, upload and download speeds are the same. This is often used in businesses that need fast uploads.

00:36.781 --> 00:48.750
This is far more expensive than ADSL, Asynchronous DSL. Speaking of ADSL, ADSL is the most common form of DSL used in homes and businesses.

00:48.750 --> 00:55.552
ADSL has different upload and download speeds, with download speeds being much higher than upload speeds.

00:55.552 --> 01:01.209
Another common means of high-speed connection for a residence or business is that of a cable modem.

01:01.209 --> 01:09.394
Besides the obvious difference of using a cable TV provider for an Internet connection rather than a phone company for DSL, there is one other major difference.

01:09.394 --> 01:18.250
Cable Internet tends to run over shared bandwidth, meaning that customers in a concentrated area are sharing the same bandwidth pipeline.

01:18.250 --> 01:29.589
As a result, speeds tend to be slower during peak usage hours, which for many is early- to mid-evening. That said, cable modem speeds tend to be faster overall than for that of DSL.

01:29.589 --> 01:38.008
The most important takeaway for the exam is that DSL connections are on dedicated lines and cable modem connections are typically not.

